  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 356 PM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nTonight and Friday:\n\nRelatively quiet conditions persist. Rain chances dwindled \nalthough there may be enough moisture convergence tonight near \nMN/WI Highway 70 to support a few showers. Isolated patches of \ndense fog are possible near the Iron Range, North Shore, and \nportions of NW WI. Strong northeast winds are forecast over \nwestern Lake Superior Friday which will increase the risk of rip\ncurrents near the Twin Ports including the beaches of Duluth \nand Superior. A Beach Hazards Statement will eventually be \nneeded. Temperatures will be near to slightly above normal with \nhighs in the mid 70s to mid 80s away from Lake Superior. Onshore\nflow and a lake breeze near the lake will keep temperatures \ncooler in those locations.\n\nFriday night through Saturday night:\n\nA weak area of low pressure will propagate northeastward from \nthe northern Plains across the Upper Mississippi River Valley. \nTwo rounds of showers and perhaps a few isolated storms are \npossible. Saturday afternoon and evening has a small potential \nfor a few strong storms. NAM and NAMNest guidance both produce a\nnarrow ribbon of MLCAPE of 1000-1500 J/kg though the GFS has a \ndeeper moist adiabatic layer and virtually no instability. Long \nroughly straight hodographs seem to support a modest potential \nfor large hail and perhaps a few strong wind gusts. Forcing \nappears broad and unfocused and with limited instability \nanything other than isolated storms would limit the potential \nfor severe weather. Highs will trend a little cooler thanks to \nthe loitering cloud cover from the overnight showers and storms \nand will top out in the low 70s along the North Shore to the \nupper 70s for portions of central Minnesota and northwest \nWisconsin.\n\nSunday through Monday:\n\nHigh pressure will slowly drift through the region Sunday and \nMonday which will provide quieter conditions. Highs remain near \nnormal in the low 70s to low or middle 80s.\n\nMonday night through Thursday night:\n\nSeveral rain and storm chances return for the extended period. \nA cold front will sag southward across the region Tuesday. \nStorms may develop in the eastern Dakotas Monday afternoon and \nevening and could move into the Northland overnight which would \nbring the first chance of showers and storms. The next chance of\nprecipitation would be along the cold front Tuesday. The front \nwill stall out on Wednesday and may lift northward again as a \nwarm front Wednesday night and Thursday, which would support \nanother chance of showers and storms. Temperatures during the \nperiod trend toward the cooler portion of the normal range with \nhighs in the low to upper 70s.",
